===========================================================================================
2. How each discriminator SCALES with field count
===========================================================================================
discriminator                      3 fields   8 fields    per field
tuple (tag + arity)                     +13        +13         +0.0
map, exact field set                    +29        +34         +1.0
tagged map (one map_get)                +14        +14         +0.0

Bytes of the Code chunk. 'per field' is the slope between the two widths.
Ticket 18 emits this guard on EVERY exported function taking a record, and
unconditionally wherever the value feeds a codegen obligation, so a slope
that is not flat is a cost the whole language pays.

===========================================================================================
3. Match and projection time (3-field record)
===========================================================================================
The ticket asserts maps are 'slower to match'. Measured, not assumed.
Three runs; read a difference only if it exceeds the spread WITHIN a row.

variant                                   run 1        run 2        run 3      ns/call
tuple  element/3, unguarded               19498        18607        18759         9.30
tuple  element/3, guarded                 18740        18887        19639         9.37
map    map_get,   unguarded               20185        19911        20398         9.96
map    map_get,   guarded                 21027        20903        20911        10.45
map    map_get,   guarded+values          23967        23258        24528        11.63
tagged map,       guarded                 21150        24592        21058        10.53

Microseconds for the whole loop; ns/call from the fastest of the three.

===========================================================================================
4. Term-model facts the erasure choice turns on
===========================================================================================
--- 4.1 does a map PATTERN match a map with extra keys? ---
  #{id := _, total := _, status := _} vs a 4-key map : matched
  => a map pattern is OPEN. Ticket 27 sec7 declined row polymorphism, so a
     record type is an EXACT field set -- which the pattern alone cannot say.
  same map, with map_size(X) =:= 3 added               : no_match

--- 4.2 does a TUPLE pattern match a wider tuple? ---
  {order, _, _} vs {order, 1, 2, 99}                   : no_match
  => a tuple pattern is CLOSED. Arity is exact for free.

--- 4.3 map_get/2 in a guard, on an ABSENT key ---
  guard `map_get('__type__', X) =:= order`, key absent : guard_failed_silently
  same guard, key present and equal                    : matched
  => it fails the guard rather than raising, so ONE map_get is a complete
     discriminator for a tagged map: no is_map, no map_size, no is_map_key.

--- 4.4 is field ORDER observable in each erasure? ---
  #{b => 1, a => 2} =:= #{a => 2, b => 1}             : true
  {order,1,2}       =:= {order,2,1}                   : false
  => ticket 09 says a record type IS its field SET. A set has no order, so a
     tuple erasure must invent a canonical one and put it in the wire format.

--- 4.5 what an Elixir struct looks like coming in (ticket 06) ---
  an Elixir %Order{} term                             : #{id => 1,total => 2,
                                                          '__struct__' =>
                                                              'Elixir.Order'}
  map_size                                            : 3
  => it is a map with one extra key. Under a map erasure it arrives as a
     record with an extra field; under a tuple erasure it needs conversion.

===========================================================================================
5. json:encode/1 -- ticket 16 sec4's generated encoder
===========================================================================================
Ticket 16 established json:encode/1 REFUSES tuples at any depth, and made the
encoder a codegen obligation. A record is the main thing anyone serialises.

  map    #{id=>1,total=>2,status=>open}    -> <<"{\"id\":1,\"status\":\"open\",\"total\":2}">>
  tuple  {order,1,2}                       -> {caught,error,unsupported_type}
  tagged map (__type__ key)                -> <<"{\"id\":1,\"total\":2,\"__type__\":\"order\"}">>
  nested: map holding a tuple              -> {caught,error,unsupported_type}

################ 18a (guard cost) - size sections ################

===========================================================================================
PROTOTYPE 18a -- cost of a defensive guard on an exported function
Erlang/OTP 28 [erts-16.0] [source] [64-bit] [smp:4:4] [ds:4:4:10] [async-threads:1] [jit:ns]

OTP release        : 28
compiler version   : 9.0
scratch directory  : /tmp/18a_guard_cost
compile options    : [{outdir,"/tmp/18a_guard_cost"},
                      deterministic,return_errors]

===========================================================================================
1. CODE SIZE
Chunk byte counts are chunk PAYLOAD (beam_lib), excluding the 8-byte chunk
header and 4-byte padding. Because the paired modules are identical apart
from the guard, the Code delta is the function's own bytecode delta exactly.
`instrs` counts symbolic BEAM instructions in the function under test only
(beam_disasm), which is attributable by construction. ImpT is the imported
MFA table -- a guard that proves a term's shape lets a CHECKED bif be
replaced by an UNCHECKED instruction, dropping the import, which shows here.

module       file     Code     AtU8     ImpT   instrs
------       ----     ----     ----     ----   ------
v_id_u        516       67       49       28        5
v_id_g        520       70       49       28        6
v_id_x        516       67       49       28        5
v_id_y        516       67       49       28        5
v_a1_u        536       74       52       40        6
v_a1_g        540       79       52       40        7
